#!/usr/bin/env python3
# luxarch:worktree hook v16 - DO NOT edit this file - it is emitted by `luxarch --emit hooks`; re-emit to update.
"""Run the fleet's worktree-per-issue lifecycle: list (and prune) worktrees, land one.

`/fleet-start` and `/wrap-up` run it, and it is wired after `EnterWorktree` (v4): creating a worktree
rewrites the shared `core.hooksPath` to an absolute path, and every run puts the relative `hooks` back
(`restore_hookspath`) and links the main checkout's ignored `Makefile.local` into each agent
worktree that lacks it (`link_site_config`). After
`EnterWorktree` it also records the entering session in that worktree (`record_entry`). The standard is
--doc FLEET-AGENT-CONDUCT-STANDARD, "Concurrent agents: one worktree per issue". Every agent works
in its issue's worktree (`EnterWorktree` name=<ISSUE-KEY>: `.claude/worktrees/<ISSUE-KEY>`), and the
main checkout is the owner's: agents change it only by a fast-forward.

  python3 .claude/hooks/worktree.py list            every worktree: issue, branch, dirty, merged, age
  python3 .claude/hooks/worktree.py list --prune    also remove each one that is clean, merged and unlocked
  python3 .claude/hooks/worktree.py adopt <KEY>     take over a worktree whose session is gone
  python3 .claude/hooks/worktree.py add <KEY>       a worktree for a subagent, recorded as this session's
  python3 .claude/hooks/worktree.py land            from inside the issue's worktree: rebase, fast-forward
  python3 .claude/hooks/worktree.py restore-hookspath   only the core.hooksPath repair every run does

`land` takes the newest base, and stops whenever what would land is not what the gate ran on: it
rebases the issue onto a base that moved and refuses, so the agent re-runs the gate, then lands. Under
`[agents] push = "agent"` the newest base is origin's: it fetches, fast-forwards the local base to
origin's, and pushes the tested HEAD to origin's base before moving the local base. The push is the
compare-and-swap: if another agent pushed in between, origin rejects it, nothing lands, and the agent
runs `land` again. Every move of a base, local or origin's, is a fast-forward, so nothing on it is
ever dropped: if the base moved underneath (the owner, another agent), the move fails and nothing
changes. Two states are the owner's and `land` refuses them: origin rewritten (its previous tip no
longer in it, a force-push), and a local base holding commits origin lacks while origin also moved.
Calls to origin carry a timeout and never prompt; the push's is longer, since it runs the repo's own
pre-push hook. A recorded `.claude/.push-hold` lands locally and
pushes nothing. With `"owner"` or no declaration, it lands on the local base and the owner pushes.

Stated limit: a rewrite of origin is seen only while this machine's tracking ref still holds the tip
it replaced. A purge pushed from this checkout, or absorbed by a plain `git fetch`, leaves no trace,
and a worktree branched before it still holds the purged commit, so its land pushes it back. After
purging origin, the owner removes the worktrees made before the purge.

Before this, agents shared one checkout and two of them read-modify-wrote the same file. git
cannot see that collision: one agent's commit silently reverted another's fix, and another commit
left out the change its message described. Worktrees made by hand were left behind dirty, on
stale branches. So the lifecycle has an owner: `land` ends in a fast-forward, and `list --prune`
removes only what can lose nothing (no uncommitted change, every commit already on the base
branch, as an ancestor or, on a branch with no merge or empty commit, as every file it changed
reading the same on the base, landed by cherry-pick or rebase, not locked by a live agent). A subagent's isolation worktree
(`Agent isolation: "worktree"`, `agent-<id>`) is locked with its parent session's pid; once Claude
Code records that subagent finished, the lock no longer holds it, and its unlanded commits are
listed as LAND with the one command that lands them. `add <KEY>` creates a worktree for a subagent
and records the calling session in it, so a running agent's worktree never reads as dead. Anything else is reported and left for the owner, because
deleting work is the owner's call.

Two things outside git's own checks also keep a worktree. A running container that bind-mounts a path
in it (a dev stack deployed from the worktree) would lose those files under it, so `list --prune` keeps
that worktree and `land` names the container; deploy from the main checkout, after landing. When docker
is absent or does not answer, prune says so in one line and goes on: the mount protects a running
stack, not work. A submodule initialised in the worktree makes `git worktree remove` refuse, so once
every other check passes, prune checks each one too: it keeps the worktree when a submodule is not at
the commit the worktree pins, holds a file git does not (`blockers` reads inside it), or its clone in
`<git dir>/worktrees/<name>/modules` holds a commit that neither a remote nor the main checkout's clone
of the same submodule reaches. Otherwise it unlinks each submodule's `.git` file and deletes that clone,
which `git submodule update --init` fetches again, and removes the worktree without `--force`. The
shared `.git/config` is never touched (`git submodule deinit` in a linked worktree deletes the
submodule's entries there, unregistering it for the main checkout). After `EnterWorktree`, each
submodule the main checkout has populated is initialised in the entered worktree.

A session dies with the machine (a reboot, a lost connection) and its worktree outlives it. The
owner starts every session in the main checkout, and Claude Code files a session's transcript under
the folder it last worked in, so from the main checkout the dead session is out of sight. `list`
therefore judges each worktree's session. It is live when a `claude` process works in it (its own
directory, or a process it started there), when a running claude entered it with `EnterWorktree` (a
session that moved on to another worktree still owns the ones it left), or when its lock names a
running holder: the lock's `pid N start T` is that process, start time included, so a pid reused
after a reboot is not it. A lock that names no holder is live too. Any other agent worktree holding work is listed as ADOPT,
with the dead session's transcript and scratchpad, and `adopt <KEY>` claims it for the calling
session with one atomic lock, so two sessions started after one reboot never take over the same
worktree.

Python 3.9 and the standard library only: this file and its helpers beside it (`_worktree_git`,
`_worktree_sessions`, `_worktree_submodules`) are installed unchanged in every repo, iOS repos
included.
"""

